WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.102
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy and Tony Award-winning,

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:06.572
Grammy-nominated actor you know
from his work in iconic films

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:07.807
such as "When Harry Met Sally,"

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.775
"City Slickers"
and "Analyze This."

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:12.244
He stars in
"Mr. Saturday Night,"

00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:14.447
which is playing at the
Nederlander Theatre on Broadway.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:16.916
It is currently nominated for
five Tony Awards,

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.519
including Best Leading Actor
in a Musical

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:20.953
and Best Book of a Musical.

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:22.055
Please welcome to the show

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:24.590
the legendary Billy Crystal,
everybody!

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:25.958
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:32.532 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:39.138 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:42.308
I'm wonderful. Congratulations.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.344
This is -- Not only
are you making

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:45.978
your musical comedy debut --

00:00:45.978 --> 00:00:47.881
-At 74.
-At 74.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.448
-Yeah.
-A little bit later than most.

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.150
-Yeah. Yeah, I know.
It's been thrilling.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.853
-And you also -- five nominees,
including Lead Actor,

00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:57.190
Best Musical, and Best Book,
which is a big deal too

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.325
because obviously that is,
you know, that's the comedy.

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:01.894
That's the the backbone
of the show.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:04.597
-We did this movie in 1991

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:06.632
with Lowell Ganz
and Babaloo Mandel.

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:10.603
And then this sort of came up,

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.438
and we kept working on it
and working on it.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.641
We wrote it together
and here we are.

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:17.509
It's been a great hit,
and we love doing it

00:01:17.509 --> 00:01:19.578
and to do it with them --

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:22.448
You know, when we wrote
the movie, we were 41.

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:24.050
-Yeah.
-We're not anymore.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.384
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:27.520
-So to get a nomination for
the three of us

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.756
for the Best Book of a Musical
is thrilling for us.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:32.492
-And the other, of course,
fascinating thing is

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.393
when you made this movie
in 1991, you were telling

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.763
the whole life of Buddy Young
Jr., who is this comedian.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:41.367
And you had to age up
a great deal in the film.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.938
-Yeah, plus I directed it, so I
was in six hours of makeup a day

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:50.076
for 54 of the 72-day shoot,
you know, to be 75 years old.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.444
Now I just show up.
-Yeah.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.546
-Which is, like,
it's very thrilling.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.315
They actually have to make me
look younger sometimes.

00:01:56.315 --> 00:02:00.386
No, it's a great -- it's a great
character to play,

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.121
you know,
because we love comedians.

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:06.493
And to do it live is amazing
because he's abrasive sometimes.

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.228
He's always hilarious.
He's confused.

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.564
He's his worst enemy.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:16.903
And to get that from the
audience instantly is thrilling.

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.871
And also to have David Paymer,

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:20.840
who played my brother
in the film,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.541
plays my brother Stan
in the show.

00:02:22.541 --> 00:02:24.443
He was nominated for an Oscar
for the film,

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:25.745
for Best Supporting Actor.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.446
So we're like a pair of
old shoes together,

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:29.582
and I mean "old" shoes.
-Yeah.

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:31.384
Well, it is -- I mean,
it's really sweet

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:34.253
that it's not just the writing
team -- Ganz and Mandel.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:36.422
It's also David Paymer.
It's also yourself.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:40.059
Are there times, again, being
a debutante at 74 onstage,

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:42.428
do you ever feel your age
when you're onstage?

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:44.864
-No. Today I do.
-Okay.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:46.899
-Well, Broadway schedules
are insane.

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:48.067
-Insane.
-Insane.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.636
-This is your one day off,
we should note.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:53.405
-Yeah, so from Friday to Sunday,
we do four shows in 36 hours.

00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:56.743
-Yeah.
-And it's a lot.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.778
The audiences have been
phenomenal.

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:01.680
I can't tell you, at this point
in my life and my career,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.317
to feel so excited about doing
something every night?

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.352
-Yeah.
-It's a really cool thing.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.420
-Can you clock right away
what kind of audience

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.089
you have on any given night?
Have you been doing the show

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.591
long enough to see
the different kinds,

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.225
or have they been
pretty consistent?

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:14.426
-They've been phenomenal.

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:16.929
But there are some nights,
you know, ugh.

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:19.532
Ohh.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:21.634
Ugh, ugh.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.103
Because when it's work,
it's not fun.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.305
-Right.
-When it's fun, it's great fun.

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:29.976
-And then I would imagine,
especially not only being

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:32.077
in a comedy but being in
a comedy that you created

00:03:32.077 --> 00:03:34.713
the character, you worked on the
script, I imagine you can let it

00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:38.017
breathe in a way on the nights
where it's really working.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.153
The audience can probably
elevate it beyond

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.654
what it even is on the page.

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:43.690
-Yeah, that's a good point
because we have a lot of fun.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.094
And when it cooks, it takes off.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:52.398
And I can't imagine a show
on Broadway being this funny

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.368
consistently like this.
It's great.

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:57.970
-Obviously, you have a movie
that you work on a great deal.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.473
It has a movie premiere where
you're sitting in the audience.

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:02.175
-Right.
-Broadway shows have a premiere.

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.343
It's a whole different thing

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:04.777
because you also have to work
that night.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:05.812
It's not just sit and watch.
-Yeah.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:07.246
-Do you prefer one or the other?

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:08.915
-Oh, I prefer the Broadway
thing.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.316
-Yeah.
-Because you got to bring it.

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:12.284
-Yeah.
-With a movie premiere, like --

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.854
If you're an actor, you did it
maybe the year before.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.188
Maybe six months before.

00:04:16.188 --> 00:04:17.723
If you directed it,
maybe you just finished it

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:19.024
a couple of months before.

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:20.326
You know, and then you just
watch it.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.328
Then you go, "I wish I could
change it, but I can't."

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:23.929
You have to bring at Broadway.

00:04:23.929 --> 00:04:26.165
And there's a lot of big people
in the audience,

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:30.136
and word goes out fast
if it didn't go well.

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.637
It's like,
"You know, it's not that good."

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:32.738
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:34.573
And it hits the streets
really quick.

00:04:34.573 --> 00:04:36.775
We had a phenomenal
opening night.

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.577
-This is really cool because,
of course, I --

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:41.380
When I first became aware of
you, it was on "SNL,"

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.150
so I knew you first and foremost
as a live performer,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.752
but you had grandkids
who came to your show

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:47.486
who never had seen you
perform live.

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:50.923
-Right. Yeah, the girls are 18
and 16, the boys are 12 and 9.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:54.427
And they came in from L.A.

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:57.697
They knew me mostly in
the beginning as Mike Wazowski.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:59.898
You know, from --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:59.898 --> 00:05:03.269
So they would call the house,
I'd go "Hello?"

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:07.506
"Oh, is Mike there?"
"Yeah. Hold on, I'll get him."

00:05:07.506 --> 00:05:10.409
[ as Mike Wazowski ]
"Oh, hi, how is everybody?
Everyone okay?"

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:12.577
[ Applause ]

00:05:12.577 --> 00:05:13.912
[ Normal voice ] Yeah.

00:05:13.912 --> 00:05:15.814
-It's interesting to me
because --

00:05:15.814 --> 00:05:17.817
I'm glad to hear that because
obviously, I have --

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:21.353
A great many of my friends
have done voiceover work,

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:24.223
and they say their kids hate it
when they do the voices

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:25.792
of the characters
they play in movies.

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:27.593
Maybe it's better if it skips
a generation

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:28.928
and it's a grandparent.
-Maybe. Maybe.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:31.196
Yeah, but then they hit on
"Princess Bride"

00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:32.498
because those are the things
I could show them.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.333
-Sure.
-I couldn't explain to the girls

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:37.737
why Meg is screaming in
the deli when they're young.

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:40.606
"Oh, no, the pastrami was spicy.

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:43.910
"Yes, yes! I don't like this!
Yes, I don't like this!"

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:45.445
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.146
-For their wedding day,
you can tell them.

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:48.914
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:48.914 --> 00:05:51.651
Yeah, no, it's been -- So it was
thrilling to have them there

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:53.486
with my daughters.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:55.988
-You also had -- Talk about some
big names in the audience.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:57.956
You had Marty and Steve.
-Yeah.

00:05:57.956 --> 00:06:00.126
-It must be nice.
I mean, not just --

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:02.127
[ Applause ]

00:06:02.127 --> 00:06:05.397
Not just comedians you respect,
but guys, you know, who are of

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:07.433
your generation, when they come
and see that like that.

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.935
-Yeah, they were great.
And Tina Fey was there,

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:14.540
and a lot of other amazing
people who I so respect.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.476
And to have them come to, you
know, see the show was great.

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:19.978
-And I didn't realize Buddy was
originated --

00:06:19.978 --> 00:06:22.648
I knew him first from the film.
-Right.

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.550
-But he was a character
you started doing

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.351
at "SNL" back in the day.
-Yeah, I actually did it before

00:06:26.351 --> 00:06:29.989
on a special called
"A Comic's Line" on HBO.

00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:31.690
And he was only
like for 30 seconds,

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.226
a little insult-comic sketch
that we did.

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:36.729
And then I brought him here,
right down the block.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:38.397
-Yeah.

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:42.267
-He was an insult comic
and we did him on the news desk.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:44.437
So I was like
a restaurant critic.

00:06:44.437 --> 00:06:46.805
And I would put down these
restaurants like Don Rickles

00:06:46.805 --> 00:06:49.609
with a bad toupee and so forth.

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:52.745
And then one night,
our great producer --

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:55.047
because Lorne was not there
the year that I did the show --

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:56.081
Dick Ebersol.
-Yeah.

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:57.416
-Who was at the show yesterday.

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:58.751
It was so great to see him
again.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.753
And we talked about it.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:04.156
Chris Guest was the anchor
on "Update."

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:08.327
And so Dick came to me and said,
"Johnny Cash and June are

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:11.330
in the audience with Waylon
Jennings and Christopher Reeve.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.598
Do you want me to put them
near the news desk

00:07:13.598 --> 00:07:17.236
in case you want to leave
the desk and go Rickle them?"

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:19.772
You know? So I said, "Great."

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.340
So I was doing
my restaurant review,

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.741
and it was a bad restaurant.

00:07:22.741 --> 00:07:24.243
It was funny stuff
and funny stuff.

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.979
And I just grabbed the hand mike
and went after them

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.948
in the audience, and just --
we just improvised.

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:30.416
It was just great.
It was really funny.

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:34.086
-That's fantastic, and so unlike
the "SNL" I knew.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:36.355
And I have more questions about
that improv style.

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.224
We'll be right back with more
from Billy Crystal.

